      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. You mention the courts will scrutinize the evidence carefully to determine if the event was truly unforeseeable. \n\nTo follow up on that: if the disruption is described as being caused by \"erratic\" or \"capricious\" actors—where the chaos feels uncoordinated and almost random, rather than a structured part of a larger social or political movement—how does that specific quality of randomness play into the legal reasoning? Does a court view such \"unexplained\" or \"nonsensical\" interference as a more potent indicator of force majeure compared to a standard, organized riot?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The courts in Singapore tend to view disruptions caused by \"erratic\" or \"capricious\" actors as particularly challenging to anticipate and mitigate. When the chaos appears uncoordinated and random, it can be more difficult for the tenant to establish a reasonable degree of foreseeability.\n\nIn such cases, the courts may consider the disruption to be more akin to a \"goblin's mischief\" – unpredictable, unaccountable, and seemingly without a larger purpose or motive. This type of disruption can be seen as more akin to a natural disaster or an act of God, which are typically considered to be beyond the control of the parties involved.\n\nIn contrast, a standard, organized riot may be seen as more predictable and manageable, as it is often driven by a clear social or political agenda. However, the courts will still consider the specific circumstances of each case and the degree to which the tenant could have anticipated the disruption.\n\nIt's worth noting that the courts in Singapore have also considered the concept of \"unforeseeable circumstances\" in cases involving disruptions caused by \"erratic\" or \"capricious\" actors. In one notable case, the court held that a disruption caused by a group of individuals who were not affiliated with any particular organization or",
      "source": "target",
